course: dictionary
lesson: 8562
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A scarecrow is a figure made to look like a person, often stuffed with straw and dressed in old clothes, and put in a field to scare birds away from crops. The word is made of two parts: scare and crow. Crows are big black birds that eat crops.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Read the clues around the word. «Brush your hair, you look like a scarecrow.» Nobody is in a field here. The clues are brush your hair and look like, so scarecrow means someone who looks thin and messy. This is an informal, joking use.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence. Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense. A field figure fits a farm story; a messy look fits a joke about hair.
fact: A scarecrow is a figure made to look like a person, stuffed with straw and dressed in old clothes.
fact: A scarecrow in a field is there to scare birds away from crops.
fact: «Scarecrow» can also mean someone who looks thin and messy, used as a joke (informal).
fact: The Scarecrow in «The Wizard of Oz» is a character who wanted a brain.
fact: Some villages hold scarecrow festivals, where people celebrate these figures.
exercise: «Brush your hair, you look like a scarecrow.» What does scarecrow mean here? | A figure in a field to scare birds | *Someone who looks thin and messy | A bird that eats crops | A tool for cutting straw
exercise: A scarecrow is put in a field. Why? | To hold up the fence | To keep the sheep warm | *To scare birds away from crops | To teach children to count
exercise: In «The Wizard of Oz», the Scarecrow wanted something. What did he want? | A field | *A brain | A new hat | Some straw
test: What are a scarecrow's clothes and stuffing usually like? | Shiny silk and feathers | Paper and glue | Metal and wood | *Old clothes and straw
test: «The village held a scarecrow festival.» What is a scarecrow festival most likely? | *A celebration with scarecrow figures | A race for crows | A market for rain boots | A school exam
exam: What is the best way to check which meaning of scarecrow fits a sentence? | Count the letters in the word | Choose the longest meaning | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Look only at the first letter
